var tafsirData_68 = {
  "1": "\"Nũn. By the pen and what everyone writes!\" opens Surah al-Qalam with a mysterious letter and a powerful oath. The single letter “Nun” belongs to the disconnected letters like Alif-Lam-Mim that capture attention and affirm the Quran’s miraculous nature, composed of familiar alphabet yet inimitable, humbling those who thought they could extinguish light with mouths.\n\n“By the pen and what everyone writes!” swears by the instrument of knowledge. Pen, or “qalam,” writes destiny in the well-preserved Record where every calamity or blessing was written before creation, writes revelation as Light that brings from darkness, writes the balance sent so people may administer justice, and writes the witnesses’ true testimony for Allah in divorce limits. What everyone writes includes angels writing deeds while you conceal or reveal, and humans writing contracts, knowledge, and histories that will be informed on the Day gathering. Swearing by pen honors literacy, teaching, and preservation, directly countering “Ancient fables!” said when revelations are recited. Ancient fables accusation dismisses writing as old tales; the oath elevates writing as divine means to preserve truth.\n\nThe pair “Nun. By the pen” also connects to “He taught by the pen” and to the messenger who recites and teaches Book and wisdom among the unlettered, showing that the illiterate prophet’s Book comes through taught writing, not invented fables. It parallels the mountain that would be humbled by the Quran; the pen that would humble the mountain’s record is sworn by.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat “Nun” as a star or whale deity. Classical tafsir leaves disconnected letters to Allah’s knowledge while noting their challenge, not assigning fanciful meanings.\n\nFor reflection, honour the pen by writing truthfully, bearing true testimony, recording trusts and covenants honestly, and teaching the Reminder that would humble a mountain, so your writing becomes part of the light that is perfected and of the never-ending reward promised next, rather than ancient fables dismissed and then regretted when proven true.",
  "2": "\"By the grace of your Lord, you ˹O Prophet˺ are not insane.\" follows the oath by the pen and what everyone writes with a direct defense of the Prophet. The verse addresses the core slander that disbelievers leveled when he recited the Reminder and they said “He is certainly a madman” and would almost cut him down with eyes.\n\n“By the grace of your Lord, you O Prophet are not insane” translates “ma anta bi-ni‘mati rabbika bi-majnun.” Grace, or “ni‘mah,” includes prophethood, revelation, sound intellect, and outstanding character that the next verse affirms. Insanity accusation was a social weapon to dismiss clear proofs, balance, and the Light without engaging, similar to “You are extremely astray” said to warners and “This is pure magic” when the foretold prophet came with proofs. The oath by pen refutes it with rational order: one who brings measured writing that would humble mountains, that descends between seven heavens, and that teaches the unlettered to purify cannot be insane; the One who smoothed earth and holds birds does not choose a madman to deliver the Light that brings from darkness.\n\nThe phrase “by the grace of your Lord” attributes protection from madness to divine favour, not merely personal strength, echoing that this is the favour of Allah He grants to whoever He wills and that grace is in Allah’s Hands, Lord of infinite bounty. It also ties to the previous surah’s closing trust in the Most Compassionate in whom we believe and trust.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear this as merely personal consolation without community implication. Declaring the Prophet sane validates the entire message about creation without flaw, about listening and reasoning, about wealth and children as test, and about the Inevitable Hour that will be blown with one blast.\n\nFor believers, the defense invites emulating the Prophet’s sanity through outstanding character, as next praised, guarding families, being true to trusts, and walking upright rather than crawling facedown, knowing that the All-Knowing, All-Wise Guardian who informed the Prophet by being All-Knowing, All-Aware will also inform you of what you did when you are presented and none of your secrets stay hidden, so sanity is shown in deeds sent for tomorrow.",
  "3": "\"You will certainly have a never-ending reward.\" promises the Prophet after defending his sanity by the grace of his Lord. The verse assures compensation that does not deplete.\n\n“You will certainly have a never-ending reward” translates “ajran ghayra mamnun,” a reward not cut off, not grudged, not reminded as favour to diminish, unlike human payments that circulate among the rich and are withheld to break away. Never-ending mirrors Gardens under which rivers flow to stay for ever and ever, excellent provision, mighty reward for those in awe unseen, and splendid homes in Gardens of Eternity. The Prophet’s reward includes his rank as the illiterate messenger who recites, purifies, teaches, the double mercy and light promised to those who fear and believe, and the never-ending mention of his name in the call that gathers on Friday.\n\nThe promise contrasts with the garden owners who swore to harvest early without saying Allah willing and found it reduced to ashes, tasting total loss; their expected never-ending harvest ended overnight, while the Prophet’s harvest never ends. It also contrasts with those who denied and said ancient fables, whose destination is described as evil; their reward is cut off by Fire’s roar.\n\nNever-ending also qualifies the pen’s writing. What the pen writes for the Prophet’s deeds is preserved in the well-preserved Record touched only by purified angels, not erased by mouths wishing to extinguish light.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think never-ending reward excuses the Prophet from the test of best deeds. The Quran shows he was tested as garden owners were tested, but he passed through outstanding character, patience with the Lord’s decree, and not being like the Man of the Whale who cried in distress, so reward follows striving with wealth and lives as best for you.\n\nFor followers, this verse translates into hope that sincere striving in his way, standing in solid ranks, protecting families, lending good loans, and bearing true testimony will also be appreciated by the Most Appreciative, Most Forbearing who multiplies and forgives, promising never-ending reward to those who, like him, are of outstanding character and who deliver the Reminder to the whole world, not for payment that overburdens, but for guidance that remains.",
  "4": "\"And you are truly ˹a man˺ of outstanding character.\" praises the Prophet’s character after defending sanity and promising never-ending reward. The verse defines his excellence in terms that explain why he can bear the pen’s trust and deliver the Reminder to the whole world.\n\n“And you are truly a man of outstanding character” translates “khuluqin ‘azim,” immense, magnificent moral character, encompassing truthfulness, forbearance, generosity that prefers others despite need, courage that stands firm against disbelievers and hypocrites when required and overlooks part of a wife’s disclosure when mercy is wiser, and humility that prohibits upon himself lawfully to please wives then accepts correction. Outstanding character is the lived balance of Scripture and iron so people may administer justice, the compassion instilled in followers of Jesus now perfected in the final messenger, and the trust that never delays appointed time yet forgives.\n\nThis character was observed by his community who called him trustworthy before prophethood, who saw him make room, protect families, and teach Book and wisdom, and who heard the Quran that would humble a mountain recited with measured beauty. It refutes “insane” and “madman” slanders that were attempts to cut him down with eyes when they heard the Reminder; madness does not produce outstanding character that endures.\n\nThe verse follows never-ending reward, showing character is the cause, not mere claim, as earlier best in deeds is tested, and saying without doing is despicable. Outstanding character is best in deeds embodied.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat character as private virtue separate from law. The Quran shows his character includes implementing limits for divorce, distributing gains so wealth does not concentrate, and struggling firmly where firmness belongs.\n\nFor believers, the model is explicit. Be of outstanding character as he was, guarding chastity like Mary, praying like the wife of Pharaoh for a house near Allah, preferring others like the helpers, and being true to trusts and testimony as those who pray consistently and give rightful share. Such character, by the grace of your Lord, refutes slander with lived proof and leads to never-ending reward with the same Guardian who supports His Prophet with Gabriel, righteous believers, and angels.",
  "5": "\"Soon you and the pagans will see,\" promises near clarification after praising outstanding character. The verse addresses the Prophet and his opponents together with a time marker.\n\n“Soon you and the pagans will see” translates “fa-sa-tubsiru wa yubsirun,” you will see and they will see, soon. Soon indicates nearness, as with imminent victory, help from Allah, and the torment drawing near that makes faces gloomy. Seeing here is not mere eyesight but real understanding when mutual loss and gain become visible, when the Inevitable Event is seen, when the trumpet is blown and the sky is torn.\n\nThe joint seeing shows that justice will be made public. The deniers who wished you would compromise so they would yield, who called revelations ancient fables, and who would cut you down with eyes will see the same events as you, but with opposite realization. The Prophet will see confirmation of the pen’s oath, of the never-ending reward, and of outstanding character vindicated; the pagans will see that ancient fables was not fanciful but foretelling of the raging Flame that rips off scalps.\n\nThis follows “which of you is mad” next, specifying what will be seen.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to expect immediate worldly seeing of all outcomes before the Day gathering where the disbelievers’ faces become gloomy. Soon includes historical vindication, like the garden owners seeing ruin by daybreak and confessing transgression, and the ultimate Day when none of your secrets stay hidden and you are presented.\n\nFor practice, soon encourages patience with the Lord’s decree and not being like the Man of the Whale who cried in distress, while continuing to stand in solid ranks, to protect families, and to be in awe without seeing. Seeing will come, as the well-preserved Record is displayed, so the wise prepare before seeing, turning in sincere repentance so that what is seen soon is light perfected and forgiveness, not the Blaze nearly bursting.",
  "6": "\"which of you is mad.\" completes the promise “Soon you and the pagans will see.” The verse specifies the content of that seeing, the answer to the madness accusation.\n\n“which of you is mad” translates “bi-ayyikumu al-maftun,” who among you is the one afflicted with madness or trial. Afflicted suggests not innate illness but being tested and exposed, like the garden owners tested as Meccans were tested, and like humankind created impatient except those who pray. Madness here was a label the pagans used to isolate the Prophet, saying “He is a madman” when they heard the Reminder, and implying his call to leave business on Friday, to lend good loans, and to guard families was irrational. The Quran turns the label back as a test of who is truly mad, who is lost in delusion thinking merchandise and honour expel inferior, who persists in arrogance and aversion, who withholds good, slanders, and gossips.\n\nSoon seeing will distinguish. The mad will be those who denied the warner, said Allah revealed nothing, and claimed the warner extremely astray, then heard the Fire roar; the sane will be those in outstanding character, whose reward never ends, who listened and reasoned and would not be among residents of Blaze. The same Lord who knows best who has strayed from His Way and who is rightly guided will reveal it.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to use this as insult. The Quran states it as future clarification, not name-calling. The Prophet is told not to give in to deniers and not to obey the despicable oath-taker, but not to mock; the seeing will itself clarify.\n\nFor reflection, the madness test is practical. Walk upright on the Straight Path, make room, give rightful share, guard chastity within lawful bonds, and be true to trusts, and you demonstrate sanity that perfects light. Crawl facedown, hoard wealth, and hinder with false shields, and you demonstrate the delusion that persists until provision is withheld and no powerless force helps instead of the Most Compassionate, at which point seeing who is mad will be too late to be useful.",
  "7": "\"Surely your Lord ˹alone˺ knows best who has strayed from His Way and who is ˹rightly˺ guided.\" asserts divine knowledge after promising soon you will see which is mad. The verse places judgment where it belongs.\n\n“Surely your Lord alone knows best who has strayed from His Way and who is rightly guided” echoes the earlier “Your Lord knows best who has strayed and who is guided” after asking which is mad, and anticipates the same after describing the garden test. Strayed from His Way includes those who denied the pen’s oath, called revelations ancient fables, compromised to make the Prophet yield, and obeyed slanderers and withholders of good. Rightly guided includes those in awe without seeing, who protect families, who hear and obey and spend to best ability, and who stand as helpers of Allah as disciples stood with Jesus.\n\nKnowing best is not approximate, but perfect, as with Knower of seen and unseen, Most Subtle, All-Aware, All-Seeing of everything, All-Aware of what you do, who wrote every calamity or blessing before creation and keeps account while they forgot. Knowing best also explains why the “soon you will see” is needed for humans; we do not yet see perfectly, but the Lord does, and His seeing will be displayed at gathering.\n\nThis follows outstanding character, showing guidance is not claimed by impressive appearance that impresses like planks leaned worthless, but is known by the One who tests best in deeds.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to use “Allah knows best” to avoid self-examination, as if guidance does not matter because only Allah knows. The Quran pairs knowledge with call to listen and reason, to walk upright not crawl, and to choose trust in the Most Compassionate.\n\nFor daily conduct, this verse liberates from judging hearts finally while motivating to guide them. Leave final straying and guidance to the Lord who knows best, but continue delivering the Reminder to the whole world, inviting to outstanding character, protecting families, and being true to trusts, so that when soon shows which is mad, you are seen among the rightly guided whose light shines ahead and whose faces are not gloomy.",
  "8": "\"So do not give in to the deniers.\" gives clear direction after establishing that your Lord knows best who has strayed and guided and that soon you will see which is mad. The command is to the Prophet and, through him, to believers who stand in solid ranks.\n\n“So do not give in to the deniers” translates “fa la tuti‘ al-mukadhdhibin,” do not obey those who reject revelations, who hinder with false shields, who call ancient fables, and who wish to extinguish light with mouths. Giving in would mean compromising balance so people may administer justice, softening limits for divorce, or treating wealth that should not merely circulate among the rich as acceptable hoarding. It would mean obeying the despicable oath-taker, slanderer, gossip-monger described next, whose traits make giving in attractive for ease but fatal for outstanding character.\n\nDeniers here are the same who denied before and tasted total loss, who said “we bear witness” falsely, and who said “how can humans be guides?” Their wish is that you compromise so they would yield, as next stated, suggesting mutual concession where truth is halved. Not giving in protects the Reminder that would humble a mountain and the Light that brings from darkness.\n\nThe verse follows “your Lord knows best who is guided,” indicating that not giving in does not require the Prophet to judge hearts finally; he is to hold the line while leaving ultimate guidance to the All-Knowing.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read “do not give in” as call to harshness toward peaceful people with whom kindly, fair dealing is commanded. Deniers here are persistent rejecters who actively hinder, not the peaceful who neither fight nor drive you out.\n\nFor practice, giving in today might be softening a true testimony for Allah to please an audience, withholding the good loan to avoid mockery, or falling for fanfare and merchandise over remembrance. Not giving in is to recite, purify, teach, make room, and spend, trusting that the Almighty, All-Forgiving who created death and life to test which is best in deeds will honour firmness that preserves the Straight Path.",
  "9": "\"They wish you would compromise so they would yield ˹to you˺.\" explains the motive behind the deniers’ pressure not to be given in to. The verse reveals a negotiation tactic masked as tolerance.\n\n“They wish you would compromise so they would yield to you” translates “waddu law tudhinu fa-yudhinun,” they wish you would smooth over, be pliable, soften the Reminder, so they would smooth over and appear to yield. Compromise here is “mudahanah,” oiling, making faith slippery, trimming the Balance so people may not administer justice, redefining limits for divorce or gains to suit the rich, or rebranding ancient fables dismissal as respect. Deniers propose mutual compromise, you soften monotheism, they soften hostility, suggesting a middle way that would keep merchandise flowing and fanfare alive without fully abandoning idols.\n\nThe wish shows they recognize the Prophet’s outstanding character and never-ending reward are attractive, so they seek to co-opt rather than confront, as with hypocrites who said “we bear witness” and those who offered to yield if the Prophet compromised. Their yielding would be performative, as with planks leaning and false oaths as shields, not true listening and reasoning.\n\nThis follows “do not give in” to show why firmness matters. Compromise on core truth would extinguish light with mouths and make soon seeing which is mad unclear.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat all compromise as forbidden. The Quran permits kindly, fair dealing with those who do not fight, courtesy in nursing consultation, and making room, all forms of healthy accommodation. Forbidden compromise is on the Light, on “there is no god except Him” and on the Reminder that perfects light, on the limits that protect families, and on truth that would humble a mountain.\n\nFor believers, the verse teaches discernment. Welcome respectful dialogue and mutual help within justice, but do not soften the pen’s writing, the Subtle, All-Aware’s limits, or the call to Friday remembrance for temporary yield. Trust that the Most Compassionate who holds birds and the Best Provider who gives flowing water will provide better than a compromised yield, and that outstanding character is preserved by not oiling truth into delusion.",
  "10": "\"And do not obey the despicable, vain oath-taker,\" begins a portrait of a type not to be obeyed after commanding not to give in to deniers who wish compromise. The verse names a cluster of traits, starting with oath abuse.\n\n“And do not obey the despicable, vain oath-taker” translates “hallaﬁm mahin,” one who swears frequently, readily, and worthlessly. Despicable, or “mahin,” is low, contemptible in character, not merely low status. Vain oath-taker swears as shields, as hypocrites did hindering from the Way, as despicable, like those who swore they would expel inferior and whose wealth and children will not help. This person uses oaths to appear honourable while being among worthless planks leaned, impressive in speech but plank-like in worth.\n\nNot obeying such a person applies even when their oath seems to promise yield if you compromise. Obedience here includes heeding advice, following agenda, or letting their oaths shape community decisions about spending on emigrants, making room, or calling witnesses.\n\nThis follows “they wish you would compromise,” showing the agents of wish are oath-takers whose oaths are shields. Their wish to extinguish light relies on oaths that impress.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think frequent oaths are praiseworthy religiosity. The Quran distinguishes truthful, necessary oaths borne for Allah as reliable testimony from vain, despicable swearing.\n\nFor practice, refuse to be swayed by emphatic swearing without substance, especially when it hinders the Way, promotes stinginess, or diverts from remembrance. Instead, give weight to the Most Subtle, All-Aware who knows whatever you conceal and reveal, and to reliable men bearing true testimony for Allah, whose oaths are not shields but records, and whose ranks are solid like a concrete structure loved by Allah.\n\nThe habit of frequent false oaths also reveals an inner estimate that words can replace deeds, as with garden owners who swore to harvest all and left no thought for Allah’s Will, only to find ashes. The Pen writes oaths, and the All-Aware counts them, so a vain oath does not add weight but removes it. Choosing instead to be among those true to trusts and covenants, properly observant of prayers, and who give rightful share builds a record that needs no shield, because truth itself is the shield on the Day when no secret stays hidden and when the Shin is bared and only those who prostrated willingly before can prostrate then.",
  "11": "\"slanderer, gossip-monger,\" continues the portrait of whom not to obey, adding two related vices to the vain oath-taker. The verse moves from oath abuse to speech that destroys.\n\n“slanderer, gossip-monger” translates “hammaz mashsha’ bi-namim,” one who defames by gesture and carries tales to divide. “Hammaz” is a belittler who mocks, mimics, and stabs with insults, similar to the brute who marks the Prophet’s snout in ridicule and who calls revelations ancient fables. “Mashsha’ bi-namim” walks with tale-bearing, carrying private talk for sin and aggression rather than goodness and righteousness, grieving believers as Satan-inspired secret talks do. This person turns the pen from writing truth to writing division, opposite to “by the pen and what everyone writes” oath that honours writing for guidance.\n\nThe pair shows how false oaths and slander work together. Vain oaths gain entry, slander removes rivals, gossip-mongering breaks the solid ranks that should be like a concrete structure, divide hearts that think every cry is against them, and hinder the way that would otherwise bring from darkness to light.\n\nNot obeying this type means not taking their reports as reliable, not letting their carriers shape decisions about divorce limits, distribution of gains, or protection of families, and not following their invitation to compromise.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think slander and gossip are minor social slips. The Quran places them alongside withholding good, transgression, and being brute, and warns that wealth and children will not benefit such a person against the Blaze that almost bursts.\n\nFor daily guard, do not obey the slanderer by repeating, do not feed the gossip-monger by listening eagerly, and replace both with private talk for goodness, making room, and lending good loans. Let your pen write what brings light, your speech be good and virtuous as in Gardens, and your walk be upright rather than crawling facedown, trusting the Knower of seen and unseen to expose what slander tries to hide.",
  "12": "\"withholder of good, transgressor, evildoer,\" adds three more traits to the despicable profile not to be obeyed, focusing on withholding, excess, and sin.\n\n“withholder of good” translates “manna‘ lil-khayr,” one who prevents good from reaching its place, as with “do not spend on those with the Messenger so they will break away,” and with stinginess that promotes stinginess and makes wealth merely circulate among the rich. Withholding good includes blocking charity, knowledge, and mercy, the opposite of the helpers who preferred emigrants despite need and of those saved from selfishness who are truly successful. It is the same withholding that would be lamented as “we have been deprived” when garden is reduced to ashes.\n\n“transgressor, evildoer” translates “mu‘tadi athim,” one who exceeds limits and habitually sins. Transgressor crosses the limits set by Allah for divorce, for oaths, and for balance, as with societies that rebelled and were called to severe account. Evildoer, or “athim,” is entrenched in sin, like persisting in disbelief after messengers came with clear proofs and saying “How can humans be guides?” The pair shows withholding is not passive; it accompanies active excess that harms.\n\nTogether with oath-taking, slander, and gossip, the profile is complete. The vain oath-taker secures position, the slanderer removes people, the withholder blocks good, and the transgressor evildoer exceeds bounds to take what remains, forming a coherent economy of delusion that thinks merchandise and honour expel inferior.\n\nNot obeying means not letting such a person define spending, not following excess in divorce or business, and not joining sin that hinders from the Way.\n\nFor communities, the antidote is to be generous with good, mindful to best ability, true to trusts, and sincere in repentance that perfects light, so withholding is replaced by lending the good loan the Most Appreciative multiplies, and transgression is replaced by the Straight Path walked upright, under the Most Compassionate who provides flowing water if you turn.",
  "13": "\"brute, and—on top of all that—an illegitimate child.\" completes the unflattering portrait of the despicable figure not to be obeyed, adding harshness and tainted lineage. The verse uses stark terms to mark severity.\n\n“brute” translates “‘utull,” a harsh, coarse, overbearing person, violent in handling, as with those who would unleash hands and tongues if they gained upper hand and who wished you would abandon faith. Brute force contrasts with being true to trusts and being properly observant of prayers, traits of the faithful who are held in honour in Gardens. The garden owners’ fixated early proceeding and the expellers’ thought strongholds out of reach reflect similar brute confidence.\n\n“and—on top of all that—an illegitimate child” translates “zanim,” one of disreputed lineage, whose background is marked by obscurity or whose character is grafted, not rooted. Classical commentators note this was known of a particular opponent, likely al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah, whose lineage was questioned and whose wealth and children led him to say “Ancient fables!” This detail is not general slur, but specific identification of a notorious brute whose wealth, children, and status did not purify his withholder, transgressing, evildoing character. It also signals that lineage alone does not confer honour; honour belongs to Allah, His Messenger, and believers, not to wealth-blessed brutes.\n\nThe culminating phrase shows that all previous traits—vain oaths, slander, withholding, excess—are compounded by brute aggression and disreputable root, making obedience utterly unwise.\n\nNot obeying such a person protects the community from compromising light for a yield that is merely brute pressure.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to use this verse for general lineage shaming, contradicting the Quran’s principle that no bearer bears another’s burden and that excellence is by mindfulness, as with Mary’s guarded chastity irrespective of lineage. The description is specific, not a license to insult.\n\nFor practice, recognize brute force and tainted character behind impressive wealth and children that would tempt you to think ancient fables dismissal is wise, and instead remain among those in outstanding character, whose never-ending reward is with the Lord who knows best who has strayed and who is rightly guided, and whose pen writes truth, not brute falsehood.",
  "14": "\"Now, ˹simply˺ because he has been blessed with ˹abundant˺ wealth and children,\" gives the motive behind the brute’s dismissal of revelation as “Ancient fables!” The verse links blessing misused to ingratitude.\n\n“Now, simply because he has been blessed with abundant wealth and children” translates “an kana dha malin wa banin,” if he is a possessor of wealth and children. The “simply because” captures the arrogance that wealth and children, which Allah gives as test and provision, become reasons to feel exempt from listening. Abundant wealth and children were earlier warned as potential diverters from remembrance that make true losers, as test that only Allah has great reward for, and as powerless helps that will not save instead of the Most Compassionate. This brute, like the garden owners fixated on early harvest, thinks abundance proves superiority, so a Reminder that would humble a mountain must be mere fables.\n\nThe verse mirrors “We tested those Meccans as We tested the owners of the garden,” where wealthy owners swore to harvest without saying “Allah willing,” trusting wealth and numbers. Blessing became delusion.\n\nIt also echoes “If we return, the honourable will definitely expel the inferior,” where honour was claimed via wealth and tribe, while true honour belongs to Allah. The brute’s “because he has wealth and children” is the same claim, leading to the same “We will soon mark his snout” response.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think wealth and children are themselves condemned. They are not; the Prophet’s never-ending reward includes being a man of outstanding character who was blessed, and the helpers loved emigrants and preferred them despite need, using wealth rightly. Condemnation is for using blessing as licence to deny, to withhold good, and to hinder.\n\nFor reflection, check whether abundance leads to saying “Ancient fables” or to thanks that uses hearing, sight, intellect hardly thanked. Let wealth provide according to means for waiting women, let children be raised to guard chastity and testify devoutly like Mary, and let provision be spent as a good loan the Most Appreciative multiplies, so blessing becomes gratitude that walks upright, not brute excuse that crawls facedown toward the Blaze that almost bursts.",
  "15": "\"whenever Our revelations are recited to him, he says, “Ancient fables!”\" records the brute’s response to the Reminder, showing how wealth-blessed arrogance handles revelation. The verse captures a repeated reaction, not a single slip.\n\n“whenever Our revelations are recited to him” indicates regularity. Revelations, or “ayatuna,” include the Light recited by the messenger to bring from darkness, the Balance so people may administer justice, the reminder that would humble a mountain, and the pen’s writing that preserves. Recitation occurs on Fridays when call is made to proceed diligently, in households where limits are taught, and in public where the Reminder is for the whole world. The brute hears repeatedly, yet outcome does not change.\n\n“he says, ‘Ancient fables!’” translates “asatir al-awwalin,” tales of ancients, old myths, a standard dismissal that avoids engaging evidence, similar to “This is pure magic” when the foretold prophet came with clear proofs and “You are extremely astray” said to warners. Ancient fables charge implies the Reminder is outdated, irrelevant to merchandise and fanfare, to honour and expulsion, to divorce limits and trust. It inverts the garden test: owners heard “say Allah willing” and ignored, so garden became ashes; the brute hears revelations and calls them fables, so his snout will be marked and garden of enjoyment will be lost.\n\nWealth and children as reason for dismissal shows ingratitude for hearing, sight, intellect hardly thanked. The same faculties that could listen and reason are used to label.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think ancient fables concern only Meccan pagans. Any dismissal of the Reminder as outdated—whether of wealth limits, family protection, or standing in solid ranks—repeats the pattern.\n\nFor response, do not obey this dismissal. Listen and reason so you are not among residents of Blaze who lament “if only we had listened and reasoned.” Instead, be among those in awe without seeing, whose forgiveness and mighty reward follow, who when Our revelations are recited say “We hear and obey,” lend the good loan, and protect families, so the pen writes not fables but truth that brings never-ending reward.",
  "16": "\"We will soon mark his snout.\" pronounces a concrete consequence for the brute who, because blessed with wealth and children, calls every recitation “Ancient fables!” The verse moves from description to decree.\n\n“We will soon mark his snout” translates “sa-nasimuhu ‘ala al-khurtum,” We will brand him on the nose, the most visible, sensitive part of the face that brutes thrust forward in pride. Snout, or “khurtum,” evokes an animal’s nose, degrading the arrogant posture that impressive appearance once gave, like planks leaned worthless that thought every cry against them. Marking indicates exposure and humiliation, as with “We will cast horror” on fortified hearts and “almost bursting” Fire that singles out deniers. Classical interpreters note literal fulfillment at Badr where al-Walid was struck on the nose, and enduring marking as disgrace in the well-preserved Record and on the Day when faces become gloomy and disbelievers’ faces are covered with disgrace, unable to prostrate.\n\nSoon mirrors “Soon you and the pagans will see which is mad” and “We have tested” garden owners who by daybreak found harvest reduced to ashes. Soon shows that delay in punishment, which made hypocrites ask “Why does Allah not punish us?” is not absent.\n\nThis follows “Ancient fables!” to show that dismissing the pen’s writing has a written response. The pen that writes what everyone writes now writes a mark on the rejecter.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to take marking as permission for personal vengeance or physical branding by believers. Marking is divine, executed through history, battlefield, and final humiliation under formidable angels, not through vigilante action, as with severe punishment prepared and Fire whose fuel is people and stones.\n\nFor the community, the mark warns not to be lured by a withholder who promises compromise if you yield. Outstanding character, never-ending reward, and the Reminder to the whole world are better than appeasing a marked snout. For the individual blessed with wealth and children, the antidote is to say “Allah willing,” to give rightful share, to guard chastity, to be true to trusts, and to let recitation humble rather than harden, so the mark remains not on you but on the pattern you refuse to join.",
  "17": "“Indeed, We have tested those ˹Meccans˺ as We tested the owners of the garden—when they swore they would surely harvest ˹all˺ its fruit in the early morning,” introduces the parable of the owners of the garden, and the surah turns from the dispute with the idolaters to a story that holds up a mirror to them. “Indeed, We have tested those Meccans as We tested the owners of the garden — when they swore they would surely harvest all its fruit in the early morning,” or “inna balawnahum kama balawna ashaba al-jannati idh aqsamoo la-yasrimunnaha musbiheen.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse likens the trial of the Quraysh, who were tested with wealth and with the Prophet's call, to the trial of the owners of a garden, and the believer is taught that the test of wealth is one of the great tests, for the one who is given abundance is tested whether he will give thanks or withhold, and whether he will remember the Giver or forget Him. Al-Tabari notes that the story is related of a man of the people of the Book, or of the Yemen, who had a garden and was generous with its fruit, giving the poor their share, and whose sons, when they inherited the garden, swore to harvest all of it in the early morning so that no poor person might take a share, and the believer is taught that the oath of the greedy is the beginning of their ruin.\n\nAl-Razi observes that the word “balawnahum” — “We tested them” — is the key, for the parable is a parable of testing, and the believer is taught that God tests His servants with the very blessings He gives them, and that the blessing which is not met with gratitude becomes the instrument of the test that exposes the heart. The swearing to harvest “in the early morning” is the detail that carries the meaning, for they chose the hour of secrecy, before the poor would wake, and the believer is taught that the sin of the greedy is a sin that loves the dark, and that the one who would rob the poor of their share chooses the hour when no one sees. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see his own wealth as a test, and to ask whether his wealth makes him a giver or a withholder, for the garden of the parable is the garden of every person whom God has blessed, and the test is the same.",
  "18": "“leaving no thought for Allah’s Will.” records the omission that made their oath a sin, and the verse teaches a lesson of the tongue and the heart. “Leaving no thought for Allah's Will,” or “wa la yastathnoon.” Ibn Kathir explains that the owners of the garden swore to harvest in the early morning without saying “if Allah wills,” and the believer is taught that the omission of the exception — “in sha' Allah” — was not a mere slip of the tongue but the mark of a heart that trusted in its own plan and forgot the will of God, and that the one who plans without the will of God has planned against himself. Al-Tabari notes that the scholars derive from this verse the instruction to say “if Allah wills” when speaking of a future intention, and the believer is taught that the exception is not a formula but a posture, the acknowledgment that the future is in the hand of God and that the servant's plan is subject to the Lord's will.\n\nAl-Razi observes the depth of the lesson, for the verse connects the omission of the exception to the greed that followed, and the believer is taught that the heart which forgets the will of God in its planning is the heart which will forget the right of God in its wealth, and that the two forgettings are one. The owners of the garden did not say “if Allah wills” because they had already decided in their hearts that the garden was theirs, and the believer is taught to guard his tongue and his heart together, to say the exception with the tongue and to feel it with the heart, and to know that the will of God governs the smallest of the servant's plans. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to attach every intention to the will of God, for the plan that is not referred to God is the plan that God may overturn, and the believer is taught to plan with humility and to act with dependence on the One who wills.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to make “if Allah wills” the habit of his tongue and the conviction of his heart, for the one who remembers the will of God in his plans is the one whom God remembers in his affairs, and the believer is taught to know that the omission of the exception is the first step of the fall that the parable records.",
  "19": "“Then it was struck by a torment from your Lord while they slept,” records the overturning of their plan while they slept, and the verse teaches the believer the suddenness with which the decree of God can come. “Then it was struck by a torment from your Lord while they slept,” or “fa-tafa 'alayha ta'ifun min rabbika wa hum na'imoon.” Ibn Kathir explains that a visitation from God — a fire, or a command of destruction — came upon the garden in the night while its owners slept, and the believer is taught that the decree of God does not wait upon the waking of men, and that the plan which was made in secret was overturned in the same secrecy, while the planners lay asleep and unaware. Al-Tabari notes the word “ta'if” — “a visitor,” “a circling visitation” — and explains that the punishment visited the garden at night, and the believer is taught that the night which hides the sin can also hide the punishment, and that the one who plots in the dark is answered in the dark.\n\nAl-Razi observes the contrast the verse draws, for the owners of the garden had chosen the early morning for their harvesting, thinking that the hour of secrecy would protect their greed, and the punishment came in the night that preceded their morning, so that the very time they had trusted became the time of their ruin, and the believer is taught that the trust of the greedy in their timing is the trust of the blind in the dark. The verse also teaches the believer the vigilance of God, for the garden was struck while its owners slept, and the believer is taught that God sees the plot of the plotter and answers it in the hour of his ease, and that the one who forgets God in his planning is not forgotten by God in the punishment. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to fear the suddenness of the decree, and to make his plans in the fear of God, for the plan that is made without the fear of God is the plan that the decree of God may undo in a single night.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to sleep in the protection of God and to wake in the gratitude of God, for the night in which the believer sleeps is the night in which the decree of God may fall, and the believer is taught to let his sleep be in the peace of the one whose plans are referred to God.",
  "20": "“so it was reduced to ashes.” describes the state of the garden after the visitation, and the verse gives the image of the ruin by which the owners would be taught. “So it was reduced to ashes,” or “fa-asbahat kas-sareem.” Ibn Kathir explains that the garden became like the “sareem” — the black of night, or the harvested field, or the burned and barren ground — and the believer is taught that the garden which had been full of fruit was reduced in a single night to a blackened ruin, and that the wealth of the greedy is the wealth that can be taken in an instant. Al-Tabari notes the word “sareem” and records the interpretations of it, and the believer is taught that the image is the image of the sudden end, the garden that was green at evening and black at dawn, and that the world and its wealth are like that garden, flourishing today and ashes tomorrow.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the image, for the verse does not describe the garden as merely damaged but as reduced to ashes, and the believer is taught that the punishment of the greedy is the punishment that leaves nothing, and that the wealth which is withheld from the poor is the wealth that is taken from the withholder, and that the hand which closed against the right of God is the hand that opened to the ruin of God. The verse also teaches the believer the fate of the world's abundance, for the garden of the parable is the world itself, and the believer is taught to hold the world as the thing that passes, and to store for himself what the fire cannot touch, the charity and the gratitude that endure. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to give from his wealth before the wealth is taken, for the garden that is given is the garden that is kept, and the garden that is withheld is the garden that is burned, and the believer is taught to choose the keeping by the giving.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to read the ashes of the garden as the image of every withheld blessing, and to know that the blessing which is not shared is the blessing which is withdrawn, and the believer is taught to let his wealth be the means of his gratitude and not the object of his greed.",
  "21": "“Then by daybreak they called out to each other,” records the waking of the owners and their first act, and the verse shows the heedless beginning their day in the very manner that had been their sin. “Then by daybreak they called out to each other,” or “fa-tanadaw musbiheen.” Ibn Kathir explains that the owners of the garden woke at daybreak and called to one another, each summoning the other to the harvest, and the believer is taught that the heedless return to their plan in the morning as if nothing had happened, for the punishment had fallen in the night and they did not yet know it, and the scene of their cheerful calling is the scene of the heedless in every age, who rise to their greed while their ruin stands at the door. Al-Tabari notes that the calling of the owners to one another is the image of the heedless community, each encouraging the other in the wrong, and the believer is taught that the community of greed is the community that calls one another to the ruin, and that the believer is taught to call his companions to the good as the greedy call their companions to the gain.\n\nAl-Razi observes the irony that the verse preserves, for the owners called out to one another to go early to the harvest, and the harvest they would find was the harvest of ashes, and the believer is taught that the plans of the heedless are mocked by the decree of God, and that the morning which they greeted with greed would greet them with ruin. The verse also teaches the believer the danger of the community that encourages sin, for the owners of the garden were many, and each strengthened the other in the withholding, and the believer is taught to choose the company that strengthens him in the right and to flee the company that strengthens him in the wrong. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to begin his day with the remembrance of God and not with the pursuit of the world, for the day that begins with greed is the day that may end in loss, and the believer is taught to let his morning call be the call to the good.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to hear in the calling of the owners the voice of the heedless in his own heart, and to answer it with the remembrance of God, and to know that the morning of the greedy is the morning of their undoing.",
  "22": "“˹saying,˺ ‘Go early to your harvest, if you want to pick ˹all˺ the fruit.’” records the words with which the owners urged one another, and the verse exposes the intent that had been hidden in their plan. “Saying, 'Go early to your harvest, if you want to pick all the fruit,'” or “ani ighdoo 'ala harthikum in kuntum sarimeen.” Ibn Kathir explains that they urged one another to go early to the harvest, and the believer is taught that the urgency of the greedy is the urgency of those who fear the poor, and that the haste to harvest before the poor arrive is the haste of the heart that has already decided to withhold. Al-Tabari notes the word “ighdoo” — “go early” — for the early going was the means of their sin, and the believer is taught that the one who plans his sin with care has doubled his sin, for the care of the plan is the proof of the intent.\n\nAl-Razi observes that the verse reveals the motive that the previous verses had only hinted at, for the owners of the garden wanted to harvest all the fruit before the poor could come, and the believer is taught that the withholding of the poor's share is the sin that the parable is about, and that the one who is given abundance and withholds the right of the poor has failed the test of the abundance. The verse also teaches the believer the manner of the greedy, for the greedy is the one who hurries to secure his own and to prevent the other, and the believer is taught to be the opposite, to hasten to the giving and to seek the poor rather than to flee them. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to honor the right of the poor in his wealth, for the share of the poor is not the charity of the rich but the right that God has placed in the wealth, and the believer is taught to give the right before the wealth is taken.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to examine his own haste, for the haste to gather and to withhold is the haste of the owners of the garden, and the believer is taught to replace it with the haste to give and to share, and to know that the one who hastens to the poor hastens to the pleasure of God.",
  "23": "“So they went off, whispering to one another,” records the manner of their going, and the verse adds the detail of the whisper that completes the picture of their intent. “So they went off, whispering to one another,” or “fan-talaqoo wa hum yatakhafatoon.” Ibn Kathir explains that they went off whispering among themselves, and the believer is taught that the whisper is the manner of the guilty, for the one who speaks aloud his just intent has nothing to hide, and the one who whispers has already admitted that his intent will not bear the light. Al-Tabari notes that their whispering was the whispering of the plot, and the believer is taught that the sin which is planned in whispers is the sin which has bound its partners together, and that the whisper of the guilty is the opposite of the open word of the truthful.\n\nAl-Razi observes that the verse teaches the believer the psychology of sin, for the sin that is shared in whispers is the sin that strengthens itself by secrecy, and the believer is taught that the open declaration of the good is the shield of the good, while the whisper of the evil is the sign of the evil, and the believer is taught to make his deeds deeds of the light and not deeds of the dark. The verse also teaches the believer the difference between the believer and the heedless, for the believer goes to his work with the name of God on his lips and the heedless goes with the whisper of the plot, and the believer is taught to let his going be a going in the remembrance of God. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to beware the whisper, for the whisper is the first step of the secret sin, and the believer is taught to let his words be open and his deeds be in the sight of God, and to know that the one who whispers his sin has forgotten that God hears the whisper.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to watch the manner of his going, for the manner reveals the intent, and the believer is taught to go to his work with the openness of the one whose deeds are for God, and to leave the whisper to those whose deeds cannot bear the light.",
  "24": "“‘Do not let any poor person enter your garden today.’” records the whispered command that made their intent plain, and the verse exposes the cruelty that had been hidden in their plan. “Do not let any poor person enter your garden today,” or “an la yadkhulannaha al-yawma 'alaykum miskeen.” Ibn Kathir explains that they warned one another to keep the poor out of the garden that day, and the believer is taught that the sin of the owners of the garden was the sin of the withholder, the one who closes the door of his blessing against the needy, and that the closing of the door is the closing of the hand and the hardening of the heart. Al-Tabari notes that the word “miskeen” — “poor” — is the word for the one who is in need, and the believer is taught that the poor are the guests of God in the wealth of the rich, and that the one who turns the guest away turns away the One who sent him.\n\nAl-Razi observes that the verse is the climax of the parable's exposure, for the owners of the garden are revealed not merely as planners but as withholders, and the believer is taught that the test of wealth is the test of the door — whether it opens or closes to the poor — and that the one who closes the door has failed the test and invited the punishment. The verse also teaches the believer the dignity of the poor, for the Quran preserves the word of the withholders as a curse upon themselves, and the believer is taught to honor the poor and to open his door and his wealth to them, and to know that the poor are the ones through whom the wealth of the rich is purified. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to fear the closing of the door, for the door that closes against the poor is the door that closes against the mercy, and the believer is taught to let his door be the door of the open hand and the open heart.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to hear the command of the withholders as the voice that he must refuse, and to answer it with the command of God, which opens the door and gives the share, and to know that the one who keeps the poor from his garden is kept by God from the Garden.",
  "25": "“And they proceeded early, totally fixated on their purpose.” records their departure in the confidence of their plan, and the verse shows the heedless walking toward their ruin as if toward their gain. “And they proceeded early, totally fixated on their purpose,” or “wa ghadaw 'ala hardin qadireen.” Ibn Kathir explains that they went early in the morning, determined and able in their own eyes, and the believer is taught that the confidence of the heedless is the confidence of the blind, for they went out certain of their harvest while the harvest was already ashes, and that the one who trusts in his own ability has trusted in what the decree of God can undo in a night. Al-Tabari notes the word “qadireen” — “able,” “fixated on their purpose” — and the believer is taught that the ability which is not referred to God is the ability that fails, and that the one who goes out trusting his strength and his plan is the one who will find his strength and his plan to be nothing.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the morning, for the owners of the garden went out early, and the believer is taught that the early hour is the hour of the giver and the hour of the withholder alike, and that the difference between them is the difference of the heart, and that the early going of the greedy is the going of those whom the decree precedes. The verse also teaches the believer the vanity of human determination, for the owners were “qadireen” — able — in the fullest sense, and their ability did not avail them, and the believer is taught to couple his determination with the dependence on God, and to know that the determination which is not under the will of God is the determination that will be overturned. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to go to his work with the trust in God, and to know that the work is not won by the ability of the worker but by the will of the Lord, and the believer is taught to say “if Allah wills” with the heart and not only the tongue.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to watch his confidence, for the confidence that is in the self is the confidence of the owners of the garden, and the believer is taught to place his confidence in God, and to know that the morning of the one who trusts God is the morning of the blessing, while the morning of the one who trusts himself is the morning of the ruin.",
  "26": "“But when they saw it ˹devastated˺, they cried, ‘We must have lost ˹our˺ way!” records the shock of the owners when they saw the garden, and the verse preserves the confusion of the heedless before the decree of God. “But when they saw it devastated, they cried, 'We must have lost our way!'” or “fa-lamma ra'awha qaloo inna la-dalloon.” Ibn Kathir explains that when they reached the garden and saw it blackened and ruined, they thought they had taken the wrong road, for the ruin before them was so complete that they could not believe it was their garden, and the believer is taught that the first response of the heedless to the decree of God is disbelief, for the heart that trusted in its plan cannot at once accept that the plan is gone, and the mind seeks a smaller explanation than the truth. Al-Tabari notes that their words — “we must have lost our way” — are the words of the bewildered, and the believer is taught that the bewilderment of the heedless before the punishment is the first taste of the punishment.\n\nAl-Razi observes the psychological truth of the verse, for the one who is suddenly faced with the loss of what he trusted searches first for an error in the path, not for the hand of God, and the believer is taught to recognize the hand of God in his losses before he searches for smaller causes, and to know that the loss which is the punishment of greed is not a loss of the way but a loss of the blessing. The verse also teaches the believer the completeness of the ruin, for the garden was so changed that its owners did not recognize it, and the believer is taught that the punishment of the withholder is the punishment that makes his own wealth unrecognizable to him, and that the blessing which is withheld is the blessing which is transformed into a curse. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to see the hand of God in the events that overturn his plans, and to turn to God in the loss as he turned to God in the gain, and to know that the loss which brings the turning is a gain in the end.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to prepare his heart for the recognition of the decree, for the heart that knows God in ease will know Him in loss, and the believer is taught to let his first thought in the loss be the thought of God, and to know that the confusion of the heedless is the confusion of those who had no share of the knowledge of the decree.",
  "27": "“In fact, we have been deprived ˹of our livelihood˺.’” records their recognition of the truth, and the verse shows the moment when the confusion gave way to the knowledge of what had happened. “In fact, we have been deprived of our livelihood,” or “bal nahnu mahroomoon.” Ibn Kathir explains that when they looked again and knew the garden to be theirs, they said, “we have been deprived,” and the believer is taught that the recognition of the deprivation was the first step of their turning, for the heart that admits the loss has begun to see the hand of God in it, and the admission of the deprivation is the beginning of the repentance. Al-Tabari notes the word “mahroomoon” — “deprived,” “forbidden the good” — and the believer is taught that the deprivation of the withholder is the deprivation that he brought upon himself, and that the one who withholds the good from others is deprived of the good himself, and that the deprivation of this world is the mirror of the withholding that preceded it.\n\nAl-Razi observes the shift in their words, for they moved from “we have lost our way” to “we have been deprived,” and the movement is the movement from the denial of the decree to the acknowledgment of it, and the believer is taught that the acknowledgment of the loss is the door through which the repentance enters, and that the one who stops at the denial of the decree never reaches the turning. The verse also teaches the believer the justice of God, for the owners of the garden had planned to deprive the poor, and they were themselves deprived, and the believer is taught that the deprivation which a man plans for others returns upon himself, and that the law of the deed is the law of the return. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to admit his losses as the decrees of God, and to let the admission lead him to the turning, and to know that the deprivation which is admitted is the deprivation which may be repaired, while the deprivation which is denied is the deprivation which remains.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to read the loss of the world as the sign of the Lord, for the deprivation of the garden was the sign that woke its owners, and the believer is taught to let his losses wake him, and to know that the loss which turns the heart to God is the gain that the world could not give.",
  "28": "“The most sensible of them said, ‘Did I not urge you to say, ‘Allah willing.’?’” records the voice of the one who had warned them, and the verse preserves the memory of the good counsel that the greedy had ignored. “The most sensible of them said, 'Did I not urge you to say, Allah willing?'” or “qala awsatuhum a-lam aqul lakum law la tusabbihoon.” Ibn Kathir explains that the most moderate and sensible of them reminded the others of the counsel he had given — that they should say “if Allah wills” and remember God — and the believer is taught that the wise counsel that is ignored in the time of ease returns as the rebuke in the time of loss, and that the voice of the sensible is the voice that the heedless hear too late. Al-Tabari notes the word “awsatuhum” — “the most moderate,” “the best of them” — and the believer is taught that the community of the greedy is not without its voices of wisdom, but that the wisdom is drowned by the greed, and that the day of the loss is the day when the wisdom speaks again.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the reminder, for the sensible one had urged them to say “if Allah wills,” and the believer is taught that the exception of the tongue is the shield of the blessing, and that the one who remembers God in his plans is the one whose plans God preserves, while the one who forgets God in his plans is the one whose plans God overturns. The verse also teaches the believer the value of the one who counsels, for the sensible one is remembered in the story as the one who had spoken the truth, and the believer is taught to honor the counselors and to heed them in the time of ease, so that their counsel may not return as the rebuke in the time of loss. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to listen to the reminder while it is still a reminder, for the reminder that is ignored becomes the witness against the one who ignored it, and the believer is taught to let the counsel of the wise find a hearing in his heart before the day of the loss.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to be the sensible one in his own community, to counsel the good and to say the truth, and to know that the counsel which is not heard in ease is the counsel which will be remembered in loss, and the believer is taught to let his counsel be the seed that the day of loss will harvest.",
  "29": "“They replied, ‘Glory be to our Lord! We have truly been wrongdoers.’” records the turning of the owners, and the verse shows the confession that the loss produced. “They replied, 'Glory be to our Lord! We have truly been wrongdoers,'” or “qaloo subhana rabbina inna kunna thalimeen.” Ibn Kathir explains that the owners of the garden glorified their Lord and confessed that they had been wrongdoers, and the believer is taught that the glorification and the confession are the two parts of the repentance, for the heart that glorifies God has acknowledged His perfection, and the heart that confesses its wrong has acknowledged its own defect, and the two acknowledgments together are the turning that God accepts. Al-Tabari notes that their words — “glory be to our Lord” — are the words that cast the blame upon themselves and not upon God, and the believer is taught that the true repentance is the repentance that blames the self and glorifies the Lord, and that the one who blames God for his loss has not repented but hardened.\n\nAl-Razi observes the beauty of the confession, for the owners of the garden moved from the denial of the loss to the glorification of the Lord and the confession of the wrong, and the believer is taught that the loss which produces the glorification and the confession is the loss which is already a gain, and that the punishment of this world is a mercy when it brings the turning. The verse also teaches the believer the words of the repentant, for the glorification of God and the confession of the wrong are the words that the believer is taught to make his own, and the believer is taught to turn to God with the tongue of the owners of the garden, glorifying and confessing. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to let his losses produce the confession, for the loss that is confessed is the loss that is forgiven, and the believer is taught to glorify God in the loss as he glorifies Him in the gain.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught the beginning of every return, for the return to God begins with the glorification of God and the confession of the wrong, and the believer is taught to make these two the constant of his heart, so that the loss may find him already returned and not in need of the return.",
  "30": "“Then they turned on each other, throwing blame.” records the blame that followed the confession, and the verse shows the owners of the garden turning on one another. “Then they turned on each other, throwing blame,” or “fa-aqbala ba'duhum 'ala ba'din yatalawamoon.” Ibn Kathir explains that the owners of the garden began to blame one another, each saying to the other, “you are the one who suggested the withholding,” and the believer is taught that the company of sin turns into the company of blame, and that the partners of the wrong become the accusers of each other, and that the blame which they threw on one another was the fruit of the counsel which they had shared in the wrong. Al-Tabari notes that the mutual blame is the Quran's picture of the heedless in their loss, and the believer is taught that the friendship of the sinful is the friendship that ends in the mutual accusation, and that the true friendship is the friendship of the believers, which ends in the mutual forgiveness.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the mutual blame, for the owners of the garden had whispered together in the wrong, and now they accuse one another in the loss, and the believer is taught that the sin which is shared in secret is the sin which is scattered in the open, and that the one who joins the company of the wrong joins the company of the blame, and that the only company that endures is the company of the right. The verse also teaches the believer the difference between the blame that leads to repentance and the blame that leads to division, for the blame of the owners of the garden was a blame that added the division to the loss, and the believer is taught to let his blame be the blame of the self, and to turn the blame of others into the forgiveness that builds. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to guard against the company that shares the wrong, for the company that shares the wrong shares the blame, and the believer is taught to choose the company that shares the right, for the company that shares the right shares the reward.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see in the mutual blame the end of every friendship built on sin, and to build his friendships on the truth, for the friendship of the truth is the friendship that stands in the loss and in the gain, while the friendship of the sin is the friendship that falls at the first blow.",
  "31": "“They said, ‘Woe to us! We have certainly been transgressors.” records the deepening of their confession, and the verse shows the owners of the garden passing from the blame of one another to the blame of themselves. “They said, 'Woe to us! We have certainly been transgressors,'” or “qaloo ya waylana inna kunna tagheen.” Ibn Kathir explains that they cried woe upon themselves and confessed that they had been transgressors, and the believer is taught that the movement from the mutual blame to the self-blame is the movement of the true repentance, for the heart that accuses itself has found the path, while the heart that accuses others has lost it. Al-Tabari notes the word “tagheen” — “transgressors” — for their confession now named their sin as the transgression of the limits, and the believer is taught that the transgression which is confessed is the transgression which is forgiven, and that the woe which is spoken over the self in this world is the woe that is lifted in the next.\n\nAl-Razi observes the progression of the verses, for the owners moved from the denial of the loss to the acknowledgment, from the acknowledgment to the glorification, from the glorification to the mutual blame, and now from the mutual blame to the self-blame, and the believer is taught that the repentance is a path with steps, and that the one who walks the steps reaches the mercy, and that the step which is not taken leaves the walker stranded. The verse also teaches the believer the words of the repentant, for “woe to us, we have been transgressors” is the cry of the heart that has seen its own wrong, and the believer is taught to make the cry his own, and to know that the woe of this world is the ransom of the woe of the next. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to accuse himself before others accuse him, and to judge himself before he is judged, and to know that the self-accusation is the first step of the self-correction.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught the art of repentance, for the repentance that blames the self and not the other is the repentance that God accepts, and the believer is taught to let his losses turn his blame inward, and to know that the woe which is spoken in this world is the mercy which spares the woe of the next.",
  "32": "“We trust our Lord will give us a better garden than this, ˹for˺ we are indeed turning to our Lord with hope.’” records the hope that followed the confession, and the verse shows the owners of the garden turning from the loss to the Lord who can replace it. “We trust our Lord will give us a better garden than this, for we are indeed turning to our Lord with hope,” or “'asa rabbuna an yubdilana khayran minha inna ila rabbina raghiboon.” Ibn Kathir explains that they hoped their Lord would give them a better garden in exchange, and the believer is taught that the hope of the repentant is the fruit of their repentance, and that the heart which has turned to God is the heart which hopes in God, and that the hope of the exchange is the hope that the mercy of God can replace the loss with the better. Al-Tabari notes the word “raghiboon” — “desirous,” “turning with hope” — and the believer is taught that the turning to God with desire is the turning that God answers, and that the one who turns with hope is not turned away.\n\nAl-Razi observes the beauty of the hope, for the owners of the garden had lost their garden through their greed, and they now hoped for a better garden through their Lord, and the believer is taught that the loss which turns the heart to God is the loss which opens the door of the better, and that the hand which is emptied by the punishment may be filled by the mercy. The verse also teaches the believer the manner of the hope, for the hope is joined to the confession and the turning, and the believer is taught that the hope which is not grounded in the turning is the hope of the idle, while the hope which follows the confession is the hope of the repentant. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to hope in God after the turning, and to know that the mercy of God is wider than the loss, and that the one who loses the world for the sake of the truth is given the better in this world and the next.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to let his losses become the door of his hope, for the garden that was lost was the door of the garden that is hoped for, and the believer is taught to hope in the Lord who replaces the loss with the better, and to know that the hope of the repentant is the hope that does not fail.",
  "33": "“That is the ˹way of Our˺ punishment ˹in this world˺. But the punishment of the Hereafter is certainly far worse, if only they knew.” closes the parable with the sentence that turns the story toward the Hereafter, and the verse states the general law that the parable illustrated. “That is the way of Our punishment in this world. But the punishment of the Hereafter is certainly far worse, if only they knew,” or “kathalika al-'athabu wa la-'athabu al-akhirati akbaru law kanoo ya'lamoon.” Ibn Kathir explains that the punishment of the owners of the garden is the pattern of the worldly punishment, and the believer is taught that the punishment of this world is the lesser and the punishment of the Hereafter is the greater, and that the one who is punished in this world has received only the first installment, and that the warning of the Hereafter is the warning that the parable was meant to carry to the Quraysh. Al-Tabari notes that the verse addresses the idolaters of Mecca, who were being tested with wealth as the owners of the garden were tested, and the believer is taught that the test of the Quraysh was the test of the garden, and that the parable is their mirror.\n\nAl-Razi observes the structure of the verse, for it states the worldly punishment first and the Hereafter's punishment second, and the believer is taught to measure the two, and to know that the worldly punishment, severe as it is, is mercy compared with the punishment of the next, and that the fear of the greater is the motive of the turning that avoids both. The verse also teaches the believer the condition of the lesson, for the verse says “if only they knew,” and the believer is taught that the heedless are the ones who do not know, and that the knowledge of the two punishments is the knowledge that saves, and that the believer is taught to know and to turn. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to read the parables of the Quran as the warnings they are, and to know that the punishment of the world is the shadow and the punishment of the Hereafter is the substance, and the believer is taught to flee the shadow by the fear of the substance.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to let the parable of the garden stand before him as his own story, to examine his wealth and his giving, and to know that the test of the garden is the test of every blessing, and that the one who passes the test by the giving is the one who is spared the punishment, in this world and in the next.",
  "34": "\"Indeed, the righteous will have the Gardens of Bliss with their Lord.\" contrasts the garden owners’ lost garden and the far worse punishment with the enduring garden for the righteous. The verse is brief, luminous, and placed directly after the warning.\n\n“Indeed, the righteous will have the Gardens of Bliss with their Lord” translates “inna lil-muttaqin ‘inda rabbihim jannati al-na‘im,” the God-conscious, the mindful to best ability who hear and obey and spend, will have Gardens of bliss. Righteous, or “muttaqin,” are those who were humble at remembrance, revived like earth, guarded chastity like Mary, protected families, and gave rightful share. With their Lord indicates nearness, as with those brought near, honour belonging to Allah and His Messenger, and a house in Paradise near You prayed by Pharaoh’s wife. Gardens of Bliss are the same promised to the foremost and to those who believe and do good for ever and ever, with splendid homes, flowing rivers, never out of season.\n\nThis follows “punishment of the Hereafter is far worse” to show alternative, as with residents of Fire vs residents of Paradise where only the latter are truly successful, and with the raging Flame that rips scalps vs Gardens held in honour for those who pray consistently, give share, believe in Day, fear punishment, guard chastity, keep trusts, and observe prayers.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think Gardens of Bliss are merely bigger orchards. They are with their Lord, indicating divine pleasure and light shining ahead, not just fruit.\n\nFor practice, this verse invites choosing which garden to invest in. The owners’ garden excluded poor and became ashes, then hoped for better; the righteous’ Gardens include poor via rightful share and become never-ending. Be among the righteous by being in awe without seeing, whose forgiveness and mighty reward are promised, and who walk upright, so the garden you tend is with your Lord, the Most Compassionate in whom you believe and trust, whose flowing water never sinks and whose authority holds all.",
  "35": "\"Should We then treat those who have submitted like the wicked?\" poses a rhetorical question of justice after promising Gardens of Bliss for the righteous. The verse rejects equality of opposite paths.\n\n“Should We then treat those who have submitted like the wicked?” translates “a-fa-naj‘alu al-muslimina ka-al-mujrimin,” shall We make the submitted, or “muslimin,” those who submit to Allah, like the wicked, or “mujrimin,” those who cut themselves off through sin, who withholding, transgress, and call revelations ancient fables? Submitted includes the garden owners after they said “Glory be to our Lord” and hoped for better, the disciples who said “We will stand up for Allah,” and the helpers who preferred emigrants, all who hear and obey.\n\nThis question follows Gardens of Bliss to show why that promise is just. Treating submitted like wicked would contradict the balance sent so people may administer justice, the severe account for rebels, and the mutual loss and gain Day where gainers are absolved and admitted. It mirrors “Residents of Fire cannot be equal to residents of Paradise. Only residents of Paradise will be successful” and “He created death and life to test which is best in deeds.” Equality would erase test.\n\nThe question expects “No, indeed not,” as with “What is the matter with you? How do you judge?” next.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think submitted means perfect. Submitted includes those who falter but turn in sincere repentance so light is perfected, whose sins are absolved. Wicked are those who persist in disbelief, hinder with false shields, and remain in delusion that a powerless force will help.\n\nFor reflection, the question invites self-placement. Are my daily choices submitted, making room, giving rightful share, guarding trusts, or wicked, withholding, slandering, gossiping? The One who knows best who has strayed and who is rightly guided will treat each accordingly, with Gardens for the righteous and the raging Flame for the wicked, so choose submission that the Pen writes as outstanding character rewarded never-ending.",
  "36": "\"What is the matter with you? How do you judge?\" challenges the reasoning that would equate submitted and wicked, following the question should We treat them alike.\n\n“What is the matter with you? How do you judge?” translates “ma lakum kayfa tahkumun,” what is with you, how do you judge? The phrasing expresses astonishment at flawed judgment, as with “How could you be deluded?” for hypocrites and “What is the matter with you? How do you judge?” after asking about scripture that promises whatever you choose. Judgment here is not court verdict but the internal standard by which you weigh submitted vs wicked, merchandise vs what is with Allah, and honour that belongs to Allah vs boast that honour expels inferior.\n\nThe question follows Gardens of Bliss and the submitted-wicked inequality, indicating that any judgment equating them is astonishingly poor, like thinking water will not sink or that a powerless force will help instead of the Most Compassionate, like thinking impressive planks leaned are solid structure, like thinking ancient fables dismissal is wise.\n\nIt also follows the garden parable where owners judged they could harvest all early without “Allah willing” and without poor, a judgment that ended in ashes. Their judgment was fixated purpose, total delusion.\n\nThe verse invites self-audit of judgment criteria. Do you judge by wealth and children as honour, by fanfare and merchandise as better than remembrance, by strongholds as out of reach, or by balance, Light, and flawless heavens that show no cracking?\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear this as insult rather than invitation to correct. The Quran asks to awaken reasoning, as with “if only we had listened and reasoned,” so you will not be among residents of Blaze.\n\nFor practice, improve judgment by measuring with revelation, not delusion. Judge that Gardens of Bliss with the Lord for the righteous is better than the far worse punishment, that submitted should not be equated with wicked, and that giving rightful share, protecting families, and walking upright is rightly guided, so “how do you judge?” becomes “we judge by the balance Allah sent and by the pen that writes truth, not by fables.”",
  "37": "\"Or do you have a scripture, in which you read\" continues challenging the judgment that equates submitted and wicked, now testing sources.\n\n“Or do you have a scripture, in which you read” translates “am lakum kitabun fihi tadrusun,” do you possess a book that you study? The question offers the deniers a chance to produce authority for their claim that wicked and submitted are equal or that they will have whatever they choose, as next verses probe. Scripture here could be a divine book like Torah that they carried like donkeys but failed to live by, or any record they claim gives them guarantee, perhaps like the unseen Record they claim to copy.\n\nThe challenge follows “How do you judge?” to show judgment without source is baseless. True scripture, the Reminder that would humble a mountain, the Light that brings from darkness, the Book and wisdom taught by the illiterate messenger, and the well-preserved Record only purified angels touch, do not read that submitted equals wicked. They read that the righteous will have Gardens of Bliss, that residents of Fire and Paradise are not equal, that trusting the Most Compassionate saves, and that the garden owners’ punishment in this world is not as far worse Hereafter.\n\nThe question also echoes “Or do you have oaths binding on Us until the Day that you will have whatever you decide?” and “Or do they have associate-gods to guarantee?” All are source checks.\n\nThis is paired with the earlier oath by the pen and what everyone writes. Pen writes scripture; what scripture do they read that justifies equating?\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this dismisses studying scripture. The Quran praises studying the Book with wisdom, purifying and teaching, but condemns carrying books like donkeys without living by them.\n\nFor reflection, examine what you read as scripture. Is it the Reminder recited, the Balance sent for justice, the seven heavens without flaw that invite looking again, or is it merchandise logic, fanfare logic, or ancient fables dismissal? Reading the true scripture leads to judging that submitted and wicked cannot be treated alike, and to joining the upright walkers whose light will shine ahead, rather than persisting as those who will see torment drawing near with gloomy faces.",
  "38": "\"that you will have whatever you choose?\" continues the scripture challenge after “Or do you have a scripture, in which you read.” The verse probes the content they claim to read.\n\n“that you will have whatever you choose?” translates “inna lakum fihi la-ma takhayyarun,” that in that scripture you will have whatever you select. Choose, or “takhayyar,” suggests picking without limit, as with merchandise where fanfare promises choice and as with garden owners who wanted to pick all fruit, excluding poor. The claim is that a possessed book guarantees unlimited selection, regardless of deeds, similar to thinking a powerless force will help instead of the Most Compassionate or that strongholds put you out of reach.\n\nThis follows the question whether they have a scripture, indicating they cannot produce one that reads this, because true Scripture that humbles mountains and brings from darkness to light reads that Gardens of Bliss are for the righteous, not for choosers who equate submitted and wicked. It mirrors “Or do you have oaths binding on Us until the Day that you will have whatever you decide?” which tests oaths rather than books, and “Or do they have associate-gods to bring forth?” which tests intercessors.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this denies choice altogether. Choice exists in the world where you are told to disperse and seek bounty, to move and eat, and to compete for forgiveness, but choice in the Hereafter is bounded by justice, not by wish. The delusion is that choice is absolute without the test of best deeds.\n\nFor reflection, ask what you choose in the book you read. Is it “What is with Allah is better than amusement and merchandise” or “wealth will not be out of season nor forbidden” without condition? Choosing the true reading leads to protecting families, giving rightful share, and walking upright, so choosing on the Day is not needed because the Book already guided choosing today.\n\nThis verse thus invites abandoning the fantasy scripture that promises everything and returning to the Reminder recited, where the pen writes truth and Gardens are for the God-conscious, not for those who claim whatever they choose.",
  "39": "\"Or do you have oaths binding on Us until the Day of Judgment that you will have whatever you decide?\" tests the next claimed guarantee after scripture. The verse moves from book to oath.\n\n“Or do you have oaths binding on Us until the Day of Judgment that you will have whatever you decide?” translates “am lakum aymanun ‘alayna balighatun ila yawm al-qiyamah inna lakum la-ma tahkumun,” do you have solemn oaths upon Us, reaching to the Day, that you will have whatever you judge? Oaths binding on Us would mean Allah is obligated to grant your decisions, as if despicable vain oath-takers could impose their will on the King in Whose Hands rests all authority and who is Most Capable. Whatever you decide echoes whatever you choose, showing the claim is that judgment, not just selection, is guaranteed.\n\nThis follows scripture and precedes associate-gods, completing three false sources. All three are exposed as absent. No scripture reads this, no oath binds the Almighty, and no associate-gods can guarantee.\n\nThe imagery recalls hypocrites’ false oaths as shields that hindered, and the despicable oath-taker not to be obeyed, and the oaths binding that hypocrites swore “we will never obey anyone against you” then never left. Their oaths did not bind Allah; Allah bore witness they are liars. Similarly, no oath can bind the One who wrote every calamity or blessing before creation and who never delays a soul when its appointed time comes.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think all oaths are condemned. The Quran honors oaths borne for Allah as reliable testimony for divorce, gains, and trusts, but condemns vain oaths made as shields.\n\nFor practice, do not rely on self-made oaths that promise you will have whatever you decide, like “honour will expel inferior” or “we will harvest all early.” Instead, make the oath that matters, the testimony of faith and the covenant to hear, obey, and spend to best ability, trusting that the One who knows best who is guided will judge rightly, and that on the Day the Shin is bared, only prostration from sincere awe, not oaths, will save from disgrace.",
  "40": "\"Ask them ˹O Prophet˺ which of them can guarantee all that.\" challenges the claimants to name a guarantor after testing scripture and oaths. The verse is brief, direct, and puts the burden on them.\n\n“Ask them O Prophet which of them can guarantee all that” translates “salhum ayyuhum bi-dhalika za‘im,” ask which of them is a sponsor, a guarantor for that claim that they will have whatever they choose or decide through oaths until the Day. Za‘im is one who stands surety, like a reliable witness for Allah in divorce or a sponsor for gains. The question asks for a name, a person who will stand and say “I guarantee this on the Day the Shin is bared and eyes are downcast.” No one can, because no powerless force will help instead of the Most Compassionate, no treasuries belong except to Allah, and no associate-gods exist to bring forth.\n\nThis follows the three source checks and precedes the associate-gods challenge, showing that scripture, oaths, and guarantors are all missing. It mirrors other Quranic challenges like “Bring your proof if you are truthful” and “Which of them can guarantee?” The silence that follows is itself evidence, as with keepers asking “Did a warner not come?” and the answer “Yes, but we denied.”\n\nThe verse also reflects the social context where slanderers and gossips, withholders and transgressors, all claimed guarantees through wealth and children, similar to “We will soon mark his snout” and “ancient fables.” Their guarantee was wealth, children, and brute force, but those are powerless.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this is call to debate for its own sake. It is a call to expose delusion so listeners reason, as with “if only we had listened and reasoned,” and so they return to the Reminder that is for the whole world, not to a faction’s guarantee.\n\nFor the community, the practical guarantor to seek is not a person who promises whatever you decide, but the trustworthy pen that writes truth, the reliable men who bear testimony for Allah, and the Most Compassionate in whom we believe and trust. Those guarantors lead to Gardens of Bliss, while false guarantors lead to the raging Flame that rips off scalps.",
  "41": "\"Or do they have associate-gods ˹supporting this claim˺? Then let them bring forth their associate-gods, if what they say is true.\" tests the third false source after scripture and oaths. The verse names partners and demands their presentation.\n\n“Or do they have associate-gods supporting this claim? Then let them bring forth their associate-gods, if what they say is true” translates “am lahum shuraka’ fa-l-ya’tu bi-shuraka’ihim in kanu sadiqin.” Associate-gods, or “shuraka’,” are partners attributed to Allah, idols, intercessors, or forces claimed to guarantee whatever they choose or decide. Supporting this claim means supporting equating submitted and wicked, or guaranteeing Gardens without righteousness, or promising merchandise better than what is with Allah.\n\nThe demand “Then let them bring forth” echoes the challenge on the Day to bring associate-gods who will not come, as with “We will gradually draw them to destruction in ways they cannot comprehend” and “No calamity befalls except by Allah’s Will.” Bringing forth is impossible because they do not exist as effective partners; the kingdom is His and all praise is for Him, and honour belongs to Allah, His Messenger, and believers, not to stones that are fuel for Fire.\n\nThis follows scripture and oaths and the guarantor question, completing exposure of delusion. All three are empty, so the judgment that submitted equals wicked collapses, and the earlier question “What is the matter with you? How do you judge?” is answered, you judge by delusion.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this is about debating theology in abstract. It is about lived reliance, as with “which powerless force will help instead of the Most Compassionate?” Associate-gods are whatever you rely on instead, whether merchandise, strongholds, or brute wealth and children that made you say ancient fables.\n\nFor today, the invitation is to bring forth what you rely on and see if it can stand when the Shin is bared and you are asked to prostrate but cannot. The only reliable associate is none; the only true reliance is the One in Whose Hands rests all authority, who is Most Capable, who created death and life to test, and who will admit the righteous into Gardens of Bliss with their Lord, confirming that submitted and wicked are not treated the same.",
  "42": "\"˹Beware of˺ the Day the Shin ˹of Allah˺ will be bared, and the wicked will be asked to prostrate, but they will not be able to do so,\" warns of a Day of unveiled majesty after testing false sources. The verse gives a stark scene of the Hereafter that follows the garden parable and the questions of scripture, oaths, and associate-gods.\n\n“Beware of the Day the Shin of Allah will be bared” translates “yawma yukshafu ‘an saq,” the Day the Shin is uncovered. The Shin is an attribute of Allah affirmed without likening to creation, belonging to Him whose Hands hold all authority and whose command descends between seven heavens. Baring signifies severity, urgency, and manifestation of majesty, as in Arabic idiom where uncovering the shin indicates a moment of great gravity, like the Inevitable Hour and the Day the sky is torn and eight angels bear the Throne. The Most Compassionate who holds birds now reveals a severity that even the Fire nearly bursting reflects.\n\n“and the wicked will be asked to prostrate, but they will not be able to do so” describes the test of sincerity. Prostration, or “sujud,” is the act they were called to in the world when fully capable but chose not to, preferring to call revelations fables, to compromise, and to obey slanderers. They were called to prostrate in Friday remembrance when they could leave merchandise, in family protection where they could bow in prayer, and in private where they could hear and obey. Now, when majesty is bared, they cannot, because habit hardens into inability, as hearts that deviated after veering and that were sealed after believing then abandoning.\n\nThis follows the guarantee challenge, showing that associate-gods cannot help when the Shin is bared, and no oaths can grant whatever they decide.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to imagine the Shin physically like a human limb or to argue over modality. The Quran affirms without likening, as with establishment on the Throne, leaving how to divine knowledge while affirming truth.\n\nFor preparation, prostrate now when capable. Answer the call on Friday diligently, protect families, guard prayers properly observed, and be among those who pray consistently, whose Gardens are held in honour, so that on the Day the Shin is bared, the ability to prostrate is granted and eyes are not downcast in disgrace but lifted with light perfected.",
  "43": "\"with eyes downcast, totally covered with disgrace. For they were ˹always˺ called to prostrate ˹in the world˺ when they were fully capable ˹but they chose not to˺.\" completes the scene of the Day the Shin is bared, focusing on expression and cause.\n\n“with eyes downcast, totally covered with disgrace” translates “khashi‘atan absaruhum tarhaquhum dhillah,” eyes humbled, overwhelmed by humiliation, like the gloom that covers disbelievers when torment draws near and faces become gloomy. Downcast eyes contrast with the upright walk on the Straight Path and with the lamp-adorned heavens that invite looking again. Disgrace here is “dhillah,” humiliation that follows the boast that honour will expel inferior, now reversed, as honour belongs to Allah and disgrace to persistent deniers, similar to the mark on the snout and the severe punishment prepared.\n\n“For they were always called to prostrate in the world when they were fully capable but they chose not to” gives reason. They were called to prostrate in the world, “yud‘awna ila al-sujud,” invited to prayer and submission when healthy, hearing, sighted, and intellect-gifted yet hardly thankful, when earth was smoothed to move and eat, when Friday call was made to proceed diligently and leave business, when protecting families demanded prayer. Fully capable, or “salimun,” sound, safe, able, not coerced by the Fire’s roar, yet they chose not to, preferring merchandise, fanfare, and gossip.\n\nThis explains why they cannot prostrate then. Habit of refusing hardens, as hearts sealed after believing then abandoning and deviated after veering. Ability then is moral, not physical.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read this as predestined inability without choice. The verse states they were able and chose not to, preserving responsibility, as with those who denied and said Allah revealed nothing after a warner came.\n\nFor today, the solution is present. Be among those who pray consistently, properly observant, whose Gardens are held in honour, who make room and lend good loans, so eyes remain lifted, disgrace does not cover, and prostration, practiced now when capable, is enabled then when the Shin is bared.",
  "44": "\"So leave to Me ˹O Prophet˺ those who reject this message. We will gradually draw them to destruction in ways they cannot comprehend.\" instructs the Prophet after describing the Day the Shin is bared and the disgrace of those who refused to prostrate. The command is to entrust requital to Allah.\n\n“So leave to Me O Prophet those who reject this message” translates “fa-dharni wa man yukadhdhibu bi-hadha al-hadith,” leave Me to handle those who deny this Reminder that would humble a mountain, the Light that brings from darkness, the pen’s writing. Leave here is not abandonment of da‘wah, since the Messenger’s duty is only to deliver clearly and to recite, purify, and teach, but to leave their recompense to the All-Knowing, All-Wise who knows best who has strayed. It mirrors “We will cast horror” and “We will gradually draw” language where the speaker is Allah.\n\n“We will gradually draw them to destruction in ways they cannot comprehend” translates “sa-nastadrijuhum min haythu la ya‘lamun,” We will draw them step by step from where they do not know. Gradual drawing includes increasing provision, health, and time that they mistake for approval, similar to treasuries thought inexhaustible and strongholds thought out of reach, while it is actually respite that leads to severe account if not used to listen and reason. They cannot comprehend because they are lost in delusion thinking a powerless force will help and that provision will not be withheld, until the garden is ashes by morning.\n\nThis follows disgrace at the Shin, showing two paths. Prostrators are honoured with Gardens of Bliss; rejecters are drawn gradually. The drawing is not immediate blast as with Thamud, but subtle, testing whether they will notice mercy as test, not guarantee.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think leaving to Allah means doing nothing. The Prophet is still to be patient with the Lord’s decree, not like the Man of the Whale, to struggle against deniers and hypocrites firmly, and to remind the whole world, while leaving ultimate drawing to Allah’s flawless planning.\n\nFor believers, leave results to the Best Planner, continue outstanding character, remember often after dispersing to seek bounty, and do not envy the gradual increase of those who reject, knowing their drawing is toward the raging Flame unless they turn to sincere repentance that perfects light.",
  "45": "\"I ˹only˺ delay their end for a while, but My planning is flawless.\" explains the gradual drawing just described. The verse gives the reason for respite and the nature of divine planning.\n\n“I only delay their end for a while” translates “umli lahum,” I give them rein, extend time. Delay is not neglect. The appointed time that never delays for any soul is fixed, but within worldly life, delay is mercy that allows hearing the warner, looking again at flawless heavens, and choosing to walk upright rather than crawl. It mirrors the treasuries belonging to Allah yet hypocrites not comprehending, and honour belonging to Allah yet hypocrites not knowing, giving time to comprehend and know.\n\n“but My planning is flawless” translates “inna kaydi matin,” My scheme is firm, strong, unbreakable. Firm planning contrasts with the brute’s vain oaths, slander, and withholding that seemed strategic, and with garden owners’ fixated plan to harvest early. Their planning aimed to exclude poor; His planning includes provision and test, and gradually draws rejecters from where they do not know, then if they persist, marks snouts, makes faces gloomy, and prepares the raging Flame.\n\nThis pair follows “We will gradually draw them in ways they cannot comprehend” to clarify that delay is part of drawing. It also follows “Or are you asking them for a reward so they are overburdened by debt?” and “Or do they have access to the unseen so they copy it?” showing that delay is not because the Messenger needs reward or because they have unseen records; it is because planning is flawless, encompassing both mercy and justice.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to interpret delay as approval, as deniers did when they said “Why does Allah not punish us?” and thought absence meant lie was acceptable. Delay is opportunity, not endorsement.\n\nFor practice, use delay wisely. The Most Capable who holds all authority delays your end for a while to allow being mindful to best ability, hearing and obeying, spending, and protecting families before the Shin is bared. Do not be deluded by continued provision into persisting in arrogance and aversion; instead, be among those in awe without seeing, whose mighty reward is forgiveness, so flawless planning finds you prepared rather than gradually drawn toward destruction unawares.",
  "46": "\"Or are you asking them for a reward ˹for the message˺ so that they are overburdened by debt?\" tests another excuse for rejection after explaining gradual drawing and flawless planning. The verse asks about motive.\n\n“Or are you asking them for a reward for the message so that they are overburdened by debt?” translates “am tas’aluhum ajran fa-hum min maghramin muthqalun,” do you ask them for payment so they are burdened by heavy debt? The question is rhetorical, expecting “No.” The Prophet, of outstanding character with never-ending reward from his Lord, did not ask for wealth or children as fee, unlike withholders who hoard good and transgress. His reward was with Allah, like the helpers who loved emigrants without desire for gains, and like the messenger reciting to bring from darkness whose provision is excellent.\n\nIf he had asked for reward, overburdened by debt might explain aversion, as when merchandise tempts away from remembrance. But he did not, so their rejection is not economic burden, but arrogance and delusion that submitted should be treated like wicked and that ancient fables dismissal is justified.\n\nThis follows flawless planning and precedes the unseen Record question, showing two false reasons for denial: cost and secret knowledge. Both are absent.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think messengers should never receive support. The Quran distinguishes asking for personal enrichment versus community support for those who give rightful share, lend good loans, and maintain families, all of which are legitimate, while the Messenger’s mission was offered freely as reminder to the whole world.\n\nFor reflection, evaluate what burdens you feel when hearing the Reminder. Is it truly cost, or is it the call to give rightful share, to guard chastity, to be true to trusts? The Reminder itself is not overburdening; it is way out and ease after hardship, provision from sources never imagined, and Gardens held in honour. The burden felt is selfishness of the soul that must be saved from to be truly successful, not a debt the Messenger imposed.",
  "47": "\"Or do they have access to ˹the Record in˺ the unseen, so they copy it ˹for all to see˺?\" tests the final excuse for equating submitted and wicked after asking about reward. The verse questions secret knowledge.\n\n“Or do they have access to the Record in the unseen, so they copy it for all to see?” translates “am ‘indahum al-ghayb fa-hum yaktubun,” is the unseen with them so they write? Unseen, or “ghayb,” is the well-preserved Record only purified angels touch, the Record where every calamity or blessing was written before creation, where the pen writes, and where the Shin’s Day is inscribed. Copying it would mean they possess the unseen’s knowledge of who is rightly guided, who is mad, and what is with Allah vs merchandise, and can транcribe it to justify their judgment that Gardens are for whoever chooses whatever.\n\nThe question expects “No,” they do not have it. They have no scripture reading that they will have whatever they choose, no oaths binding until the Day, no guarantor, no associate-gods, and no unseen Record to copy. All false sources are exhausted, leaving only the Reminder, which they called ancient fables.\n\nThis follows flawless planning and reward questions, showing that neither cost nor hidden knowledge explains rejection; rejection is delusion persisting in arrogance and aversion.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think the unseen is accessible through fortune-tellers whose mumbling the Quran is not, or through astrology that stars as lamps might suggest. The Quran declares it is not the fortune-teller’s mumbling, yet hardly mindful, and stars are lamps and missiles, not sources of unseen copying.\n\nFor practice, abandon claims to unseen. Do not say “I know my garden will be better without ‘Allah willing’” or “my wealth and children guarantee honour will expel inferior.” Instead, trust the Knower of unseen and seen, the Most Subtle, All-Aware who wrote destiny for everything and who descends command between seven heavens. Copy instead what the pen writes truthfully, the Reminder for the whole world, and let that be your unseen preparation, lending the good loan that the Most Appreciative multiplies, so that when the unseen becomes seen and the Shin is bared, your record shows you wrote truth.",
  "48": "“So be patient with your Lord’s decree, and do not be like ˹Jonah,˺ the Man of the Whale, who cried out ˹to Allah˺, in total distress.” turns from the parable to the Prophet himself, and the verse gives the instruction that the surah has been preparing. “So be patient with your Lord's decree, and do not be like Jonah, the Man of the Whale, who cried out to Allah, in total distress,” or “fasbir li-hukmi rabbika wa la takun ka-sahibi al-hooti idh nada wa huwa makthoom.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse commands the Prophet to be patient with the decree of his Lord, and warns him against the impatience of Jonah, the companion of the whale, who had been swallowed and who cried out to God in his distress, and the believer is taught that the patience of the messenger is the patience of the one who leaves the matter to his Lord, while the impatience of Jonah was the impatience of the one who left his people in anger before the command of his Lord, and that the whale was the trial through which Jonah learned the patience he had lacked. Al-Tabari notes the word “makthoom” — “full of grief,” “choked with distress” — and explains that Jonah cried out while his heart was full, and the believer is taught that the distress of the prophet was the distress of the human heart, and that God heard it and answered it.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the comparison, for the verse teaches the Prophet, and through him every believer, that the decree of God is to be met with patience and not with the anger that makes a man abandon his post, and that the messenger who is patient is the messenger whom God upholds, while the messenger who is hasty is the messenger whom God corrects through trial, and the believer is taught to hold his post and to wait upon the decree. The verse also teaches the believer the mercy that follows the distress, for Jonah cried out in the whale and God answered him, and the believer is taught that the cry of the distressed is heard by the Lord of the distress, and that the patience which is commanded is not the patience that never cries but the patience that cries to God and not against the decree. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to be patient with the decree of God, for the decree is the wisdom of God, and the believer is taught to know that the haste which Jonah showed was corrected by the whale, and that the believer is spared the whale by the patience that the verse commands.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to make the patience of the Prophet his own, to receive the decree of his Lord with the trust that the decree is good, and to know that the one who is patient with the decree is the one whom the decree serves, while the one who rebels against the decree is the one whom the decree corrects, and the believer is taught to be of the patient, and to cry out to God in his distress, and to leave the outcome to the Lord of the outcome.",
  "49": "\"Had he not been shown grace by his Lord, he would have certainly been cast onto the open ˹shore˺, still blameworthy.\" continues the Jonah lesson, showing what grace prevented. The verse imagines the alternative without mercy.\n\n“Had he not been shown grace by his Lord, he would have certainly been cast onto the open shore, still blameworthy” translates “law la an tadarakahu ni‘matun min rabbihi la-nubidha bil-‘ara’i wa huwa madhmum.” Open shore, or “‘ara’,” is barren, exposed place, vulnerable, like ashes after garden or the quaking earth that swallows. Cast, or “nubidha,” is thrown, similar to being tossed into the Blaze. Still blameworthy, or “madhmum,” is censured, retaining fault, as with those told “Make no excuses, you are only rewarded for what you used to do.” Without grace, Jonah’s impatience would have left him exposed and blamed, despite his prophethood.\n\nGrace, or “ni‘mah,” from his Lord is the turning point, as with forgiveness and mighty reward for those in awe unseen, provision from sources never imagined for the mindful, and ease after hardship. Grace includes his cry in darkness, “There is no god except You, glory be to You, I have been among the wrongdoers,” echoed in the garden owners’ “Glory be to our Lord, we have been wrongdoers.” That glorification, similar to the mountain’s humility, invited grace.\n\nThis follows patience command, showing why patience matters and what grace does. Without it, even a prophet would be cast blameworthy; with it, he is chosen and made righteous next.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think grace excuses impatience entirely. Grace requires turning. Jonah turned with hope, as garden owners turned with hope for a better garden.\n\nFor the Prophet and believers, the lesson is to couple patience with grace-seeking. Be patient with decree, continue delivering the Reminder that is not ancient fables, and when distress comes as it did for Jonah in whale’s belly, cry out with glorification, so grace reaches before being cast onto open shore, transforming blameworthy exposure into chosen righteousness and never-ending reward with the Lord who is Most Compassionate in whom we believe and trust, whose water will not sink if you turn.",
  "50": "\"Then his Lord chose him, making him one of the righteous.\" completes the Jonah arc after stating he would have been cast blameworthy without grace. The verse shows restoration.\n\n“Then his Lord chose him, making him one of the righteous” translates “fa-jtabahu rabbuhu fa-ja‘alahu min al-salihin,” his Lord selected him, purified him, and placed him among the righteous. Chose, or “ijtabahu,” is selection after distress, similar to the Most Compassionate choosing the illiterate messenger who recites and purifies, and the Lord of infinite bounty granting favour to whoever He wills. Righteous, or “salihin,” includes the submitted not treated like wicked, the rightly guided who walk upright, and the mindful who protect families and give rightful share.\n\nThis follows grace on the open shore, showing grace leads to election and righteousness, as with the garden owners’ hope for a better garden after turning, and as with divorce limits where perhaps Allah will bring about a change of heart. Jonah’s story moves from impatience and distress to grace to chosenness to righteousness, modeling that even prophetic error, when met with glorification and turning, becomes elevation.\n\nIt also parallels the Prophet’s own reassurance. Told not to be like Jonah in haste, he is told Jonah was ultimately chosen, indicating that patience will also lead to chosenness, and that his outstanding character and never-ending reward are secure.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think chosenness is arbitrary, bypassing best deeds test. Chosenness follows turning with hope, as garden owners turned, and as Jonah cried in total distress with glorification. It is favour, but favour that responds to turning.\n\nFor believers, this verse offers hope after failure. If you withheld good, compromised for yield, or left “Allah willing” out, you may have been cast toward ashes like the garden. Grace remains available. Cry with “Glory be to our Lord,” turn sincerely so light is perfected, and trust the Lord who chose Jonah to choose you, making you among the righteous whose Gardens of Bliss are with their Lord and whose reward never ends.",
  "51": "\"The disbelievers would almost cut you down with their eyes when they hear ˹you recite˺ the Reminder, and say, “He is certainly a madman.”\" describes the hostile reception of the Reminder after affirming Jonah’s restoration. The verse returns to the slander rebutted at the surah’s start.\n\n“The disbelievers would almost cut you down with their eyes when they hear you recite the Reminder” translates “la-yuzliqunaka bi-absarihim lamma sami‘u al-dhikr,” they would almost make you slip or fall with their eyes when they hear the Reminder. Eyes cutting is intense, envious, hateful glaring, wishing to make the Prophet stumble, similar to merchandise fanfare cutting away crowds from the pulpit and to the Fire’s glaring that the wicked wish to extinguish with mouths. Hearing the Reminder triggers the glare, as with “whenever Our revelations are recited to him, he says Ancient fables” and the brute’s wealth-blessed arrogance.\n\n“and say, ‘He is certainly a madman’” repeats the insanity charge from verse 2, where grace proved he is not insane. Repeating shows persistence, like saying warner is extremely astray and calling clear proofs pure magic.\n\nThis follows Jonah’s example to show that eye-cutting is not new. Jonah was also rejected, swallowed, and delivered, yet chosen. The Prophet’s eye-cutting is part of the test where We have tested as We tested garden owners, and where delusion thinks a powerless force will help.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think eye-glaring has independent power without Allah’s Will. The Quran says secret talks cannot harm except by His Will, and flawless planning delays yet is firm, so eyes cannot cut down unless permitted.\n\nFor the Prophet and followers, the response is not to be cut down, but to remain outstanding in character, patient with decree, and continuing to recite the Reminder that is for the whole world, trusting that the Lord who knows best who is rightly guided will protect, that the pen will write truth despite glares, and that never-ending reward remains for those who are not mad but rightly guided, walking upright while the glaring think every cry is against them like planks.",
  "52": "\"But it is simply a reminder to the whole world.\" closes Surah al-Qalam with universal purpose after describing eye-cutting and the madman charge. The verse reframes the Reminder’s scope.\n\n“But it is simply a reminder to the whole world” translates “wa ma huwa illa dhikrun lil-‘alamin,” it is nothing but a reminder for all worlds. Reminder, or “dhikr,” is the Quran recited, the Light that brings from darkness, the Balance so people may administer justice, the mountain-humbling Reminder, the Light in which belief is called. Whole world, or “‘alamin,” includes the Most Compassionate’s worlds, humankind, jinn, heavens and earth that glorify, the illiterate and those not yet joined, the garden owners and the submitted, the wicked and the righteous, the Meccans tested and all tested.\n\nSimply indicates it is not ancient fables, not magic, not madness, not merchandise, not a faction’s honour, not a brute’s wealth. It is reminder, as with “We have tested as We tested garden owners” to take lesson, “So take a lesson O people of insight” after expulsion without horse or camel, and “If only we had listened and reasoned.”\n\nThis follows eye-cutting to show that glaring does not shrink scope. The Reminder remains for the whole world despite eyes that would cut down the reciter, just as light will be perfected even to dismay of disbelievers, and religion of truth will prevail even to dismay of polytheists.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to limit reminder to Arabs or to the Prophet’s lifetime. Whole world is timeless and universal, as with “This is the favour of Allah He grants to whoever He wills” and “Lord of infinite bounty.”\n\nFor practice, treat the Quran as your reminder to the whole world, not an ancient fable. Listen and reason, protect families from Fire, give rightful share, walk upright, be patient with decree, and deliver the Reminder with outstanding character, so your pen writes what the whole world needs, and your reward, like the Prophet’s, becomes never-ending with the One in Whose Hands rests all authority and who is Most Capable of everything."
};
